LONG.update({
'free-12x36-album-psd-download':('Free 12x36 Album PSD Download Guide',[
"A free 12x36 album PSD can save hours on every wedding job, but only if the file is genuinely print-ready and easy to edit. This guide explains what to look for before you download, how to check a file once it opens, and how to turn a free template into a finished spread your lab will accept without complaints.",
"## What a 12x36 album PSD actually contains",
"A 12x36 sheet is one open spread: two 12x18 pages side by side with the fold in the centre. A well-built template is 10800 x 3600 pixels at 300 DPI and is organised into clearly named layer groups. Typically you will find a background layer or group, ornament and texture layers, photo frames built as shapes, text layers for names and dates, and sometimes a guide layer marking the centre fold and safe margin.",
"### Signs of a good template",
"- **Correct size:** open Image > Image Size and confirm 36 x 12 inches at 300 pixels per inch, or 10800 x 3600 pixels.",
"- **Named layers:** groups such as Background, Frames and Text instead of Layer 1 to Layer 87.",
"- **Editable frames:** each photo slot is a shape or mask you can clip a photo into.",
"- **Safe zones:** nothing important within roughly half an inch of the edges or across the fold.",
"- **Live text:** names and quotes are text layers, not flattened pixels.",
"## Where to start browsing",
"The quickest route is the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), which lists every free sheet at this size. If you want a specific style, the tag pages help: [Karizma 12x36 PSDs](/tag/karizma-album-12x36-psd-free-download/), [Hindu wedding album PSDs](/tag/hindu-wedding-album-psd/), [floral album PSDs](/tag/floral-album-psd/) and [golden album PSDs](/tag/golden-album-psd/). Download two or three sheets from the same design family so the whole album shares one look.",
"## Checking the file after download",
"Most templates arrive as a ZIP. Extract it fully before opening; double-clicking a PSD inside a ZIP preview often fails or opens a temporary copy you later lose. If extraction throws an error, our guide to [fixing ZIP extraction errors](/blog/how-to-fix-zip-extraction-errors/) covers the usual causes.",
"Once open, do three quick checks. First, look at the Layers panel for missing font warnings; a yellow triangle on a text layer means the font is not installed. Second, toggle the guide layer on to see the fold and margins. Third, zoom to 100 percent on ornaments and textures to make sure they are sharp rather than upscaled from a small source.",
"## Replacing photos the right way",
"Drag your photo into the document, place it directly above the frame layer, then press Alt+Ctrl+G (Option+Cmd+G on Mac) to create a clipping mask. The photo now shows only inside the frame. Convert the photo to a Smart Object before scaling so you can resize it repeatedly without losing quality. Our [clipping masks guide](/blog/clipping-masks-explained/) explains the technique in more depth, and [how to edit a wedding album PSD](/blog/how-to-edit-wedding-album-psd/) walks through the whole sheet.",
"### Keep faces away from the fold",
"The centre of a 12x36 spread disappears slightly into the binding on many album types. Never place a face, a pair of hands in a garland exchange, or a line of text across the middle. Move the photo or choose a different frame instead.",
"## Customising without breaking the design",
"Free templates look best when you change content, not structure. Swap photos, update names and dates, and adjust colours to match the wedding, but keep the margins, ornament positions and font sizes roughly as designed. If you need to recolour a background, add a Hue/Saturation or Color Balance adjustment layer clipped to it rather than painting over the pixels. That way you can undo the change at any time.",
"## Exporting for print",
"When the sheet is finished, save a layered PSD for your archive, then export a flattened JPG at maximum quality in sRGB unless your lab asks otherwise. Name the files in page order, such as 01_cover, 02_sheet, 03_sheet, so nothing gets shuffled. Before sending, run through our [print-ready PSD checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/) and the [12x36 album size guide](/blog/12x36-album-size-guide/) for pixel and DPI details.",
"## Using free templates responsibly",
"Free album PSDs are meant to be used in client work, but the design itself should not be resold as a template pack. Credit is not required for print albums, though it is always appreciated. If you build your own variations, save them as a personal library so the next album starts even faster.",
]),
'how-to-edit-karizma-album-psd':('How to Edit a Karizma Album PSD in Photoshop',[
"Karizma albums are known for thick laminated sheets, rich backgrounds and full-bleed photos, so the PSD files behind them are usually larger and more layered than ordinary album pages. This step-by-step guide shows how to open, edit and export a Karizma album PSD without losing quality or breaking the layout.",
"## Before you open the file",
"Karizma PSDs at 12x36 often weigh 300 MB to 1 GB once photos are placed. Close other heavy programs, make sure your scratch disk has at least 20 GB free, and copy the template to a local drive instead of editing it from a USB stick or network folder. If Photoshop already feels sluggish, read [why Photoshop slows down with large album PSDs](/blog/photoshop-slow-large-album-psd/) first.",
"## Step 1: Understand the layer structure",
"Open the Layers panel and collapse every group. A typical Karizma sheet has a base background, one or two texture overlays set to Multiply or Soft Light, decorative frames, photo placeholders, and a text group. Click the eye icon on each group in turn to see what it controls. Spending two minutes here prevents accidentally editing the wrong layer later.",
"### Lock what you will not change",
"Select the background and ornament groups and click the lock icon. Now stray clicks with the Move tool will only pick up photos and text, which makes editing much faster.",
"## Step 2: Place and clip your photos",
"Use File > Place Embedded to bring each photo in as a Smart Object. Position it above its placeholder and clip it with Alt+Ctrl+G. Scale with Ctrl+T while holding Shift if your Photoshop version needs it for proportional scaling. For the hero image on a Karizma spread, choose a photo with clean edges and simple background, because the heavy decoration around it will already provide plenty of detail.",
"## Step 3: Match the photos to the sheet",
"Karizma backgrounds are often warm gold, maroon or deep green. Photos shot under cool daylight can look out of place on them. Add a clipped Color Balance or Curves adjustment above each photo to warm the midtones slightly. For a consistent look across the album, see our guide to [colour grading wedding album photos](/blog/colour-grading-wedding-album-photos/).",
"## Step 4: Edit names, dates and quotes",
"Double-click the T thumbnail of a text layer to select its contents and type the couple's names. If a font is missing, Photoshop will warn you; install it or replace it using our [missing fonts guide](/blog/fix-missing-fonts-in-psd/). Keep the original size and tracking so the text still fits the ornament around it.",
"## Step 5: Adjust colours without repainting",
"If the client wants a different colour theme, do not paint over the background. Add a Hue/Saturation adjustment clipped to the background group and shift the hue gently. For gold elements, a Gradient Map from dark brown to pale yellow gives a convincing metallic tone. Our article on [wedding album colour themes](/blog/wedding-album-colour-themes/) has palette ideas that suit Karizma styles.",
"## Step 6: Check bleed, fold and safe area",
"Karizma sheets are trimmed after lamination, so the lab cuts a few millimetres from every edge. Keep text and faces well inside the margin, and extend backgrounds all the way to the canvas edge. Turn on View > Rulers and drag guides at 0.5 inch from each side and at the 18 inch centre line to check.",
"## Step 7: Save and export",
"Save the layered PSD with a clear name like Sharma_Wedding_Sheet05.psd. Then use File > Export > Export As or Save As JPG at quality 12. Keep sRGB unless your lab specifies otherwise. Our guide to [exporting album sheets as JPG](/blog/export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print/) covers batch export when you have 30 or 40 sheets.",
"## Where to find Karizma templates",
"Browse the [Karizma album PSD tag](/tag/karizma-album-psd/) or the [Karizma 12x36 free downloads](/tag/karizma-album-12x36-psd-free-download/), and pair them with matching backgrounds from the [album background category](/category/album-background/). For design ideas beyond editing, read our [Karizma album design tips](/blog/karizma-album-design-tips/).",
]),
'12x18-album-size-guide':('12x18 Album Size Guide: Pixels, DPI and Uses',[
"The 12x18 size causes more confusion than any other album format, because it can mean a single page, a closed book, or a standalone print. This guide clears up what 12x18 means in album work, gives the exact pixel dimensions, and explains when a 12x18 sheet is the right choice.",
"## Two meanings of 12x18",
"In Indian album work, 12x18 usually refers to one of two things:",
"- **A single page or sheet** measuring 12 x 18 inches, designed on its own and printed as one piece. Common for birthday books, studio portraits and smaller event albums.",
"- **The closed size of a 12x36 album.** When a 12x36 spread folds in half, each side is 12 x 18 inches.",
"Always ask the lab or client which one they mean. Designing a single 12x18 page when they expected a full 12x36 spread is a costly mistake.",
"## Pixel dimensions",
"Pixels are inches multiplied by resolution. For a 12x18 sheet:",
"- **300 DPI:** 5400 x 3600 pixels (landscape) or 3600 x 5400 pixels (portrait).",
"- **250 DPI:** 4500 x 3000 pixels.",
"- **200 DPI:** 3600 x 2400 pixels, accepted by some labs for larger prints.",
"300 DPI is the safe default. See [photo print sizes in pixels and DPI](/blog/photo-print-sizes-pixels-dpi/) for other formats.",
"## Landscape or portrait?",
"Most 12x18 album sheets are landscape, which suits group photos and wide venue shots. Portrait 12x18 works well for bridal portraits, maternity sessions and single-subject layouts. If the album is bound on the long edge, landscape sheets open naturally; for portrait sheets, confirm the binding direction with the lab before designing.",
"## When to choose a 12x18 album",
"### Budget albums and parent copies",
"A 12x18 book uses roughly half the paper of a 12x36 album with the same number of sheets, so it is a popular choice for parents' copies and smaller functions.",
"### Birthday and baby albums",
"Children's albums are handled often and passed around family gatherings. A 12x18 size is light enough for grandparents to hold. Browse the [birthday and baby album category](/category/birthday-baby-album/) for templates.",
"### Studio portrait prints",
"Many studios print 12x18 enlargements for frames. A designed 12x18 sheet with a soft background and a short caption sells well as an add-on.",
"## Setting up a 12x18 document",
"Create a new document at 18 x 12 inches, 300 pixels per inch, RGB colour, white background. Add guides 0.25 to 0.5 inch inside each edge for the safe area. If the sheet will be one half of a folded book, add a guide at the 9 inch mark only if the lab prints both pages on one sheet; otherwise no centre guide is needed.",
"## Converting other sizes to 12x18",
"You can adapt a 12x36 design by cropping one half, but the result often looks unbalanced because ornaments were placed for a wide spread. It is usually quicker to start from a native 12x18 template. Our guide on [resizing album PSDs](/blog/resize-album-psd-to-another-size/) explains when scaling works and when it does not.",
"## Free 12x18 templates",
"Start with the [12x18 album category](/category/album-12x18/) or the [12x18 album PSD tag](/tag/12x18-album-psd/). For covers that fit a 12x18 closed book, look at the [album cover collection](/category/album-cover/) and read [album cover dimensions](/blog/album-cover-dimensions/). Comparing formats? Our [wedding album sizes in India](/blog/wedding-album-sizes-india/) chart puts 12x18 alongside every common size.",
]),
'18x24-album-size-guide':('18x24 Album Sheet Size Guide for Studios',[
"The 18x24 format is less common than 12x36, but it is popular for portrait-oriented albums, large studio prints and poster-style sheets. This guide covers pixel sizes, orientation, design tips and where the format makes sense.",
"## What 18x24 means",
"An 18x24 sheet measures 18 inches by 24 inches. In album work it is usually designed as a single portrait or landscape page, though some labs use it as the open size of a 18x12 book. Confirm with your lab whether they expect a spread or a single sheet before you begin.",
"## Pixel dimensions",
"- **300 DPI:** 7200 x 5400 pixels (landscape) or 5400 x 7200 pixels (portrait).",
"- **250 DPI:** 6000 x 4500 pixels.",
"- **200 DPI:** 4800 x 3600 pixels.",
"At 300 DPI an 18x24 document with a dozen photos placed can exceed 500 MB, so make sure your computer has enough RAM and scratch disk space.",
"## Where the 18x24 size works well",
"### Portrait-format wedding albums",
"Some couples prefer a tall album that suits full-length bridal portraits. An 18x24 portrait sheet gives a single photo real presence without needing to crop the feet or the long saree pallu.",
"### Wall prints and frames",
"18x24 is a standard poster size, so a designed sheet can double as a framed print. Many studios design one hero sheet per wedding at this size as an upsell.",
"### Event and corporate albums",
"For conferences, school functions and corporate events, the larger canvas fits more group photos at a readable size.",
"## Design tips for large sheets",
"- **Fewer, larger photos:** a big canvas does not mean more photos. Three to five strong images look better than twelve small ones.",
"- **Check sharpness:** at 18x24, soft or noisy photos show. Use the highest-resolution originals, not compressed WhatsApp copies.",
"- **Scale text up:** names and captions that look fine on a 12x18 sheet can appear tiny. Body text should be around 18 to 24 points, titles much larger.",
"- **Leave breathing space:** generous margins of 0.75 inch or more make large sheets feel premium.",
"## Setting up the document",
"Create a document at 24 x 18 inches (or 18 x 24 for portrait), 300 pixels per inch, RGB. Add guides for a 0.5 inch safe area. Convert every photo to a Smart Object when you place it so you can scale it without degrading quality.",
"## Printing considerations",
"Large sheets show colour casts and banding more clearly. Soft gradients in backgrounds can band when exported as 8-bit JPG; adding a small amount of noise (0.5 to 1 percent) to gradient layers helps. Ask the lab for a test print on the paper you plan to use, and read [choosing paper finishes for wedding albums](/blog/best-paper-for-wedding-albums/).",
"## Templates to start from",
"Browse the [18x24 album category](/category/album-18x24/) and the [18x24 album PSD tag](/tag/18x24-album-psd/). For mixed-size albums, compare with the [12x18 size guide](/blog/12x18-album-size-guide/) and our [wedding album sizes chart](/blog/wedding-album-sizes-india/). If you need to adapt an existing design, see [how to resize an album PSD](/blog/resize-album-psd-to-another-size/).",
]),
'colour-grading-wedding-album-photos':('Colour Grading Wedding Album Photos',[
"Colour correction fixes problems; colour grading creates a mood. A wedding album with consistent grading feels like one story rather than a stack of photos from different cameras and lighting conditions. This guide shows a practical grading workflow for album designers using Lightroom, Camera Raw and Photoshop.",
"## Correction first, grading second",
"Grading works only on photos that are already neutral. Fix white balance, exposure and skin tones first; our [colour correction tips](/blog/album-colour-correction-tips/) cover that step. Once the photos match each other, grading adds the creative layer on top.",
"## Choosing a look for the album",
"Pick one direction for the whole album and stick to it. Common looks for Indian weddings include:",
"- **Warm and rich:** golden highlights, deep reds, slightly lifted shadows. Suits traditional temple and mandap ceremonies.",
"- **Clean and bright:** neutral whites, soft pastels, gentle contrast. Suits daytime and Christian ceremonies.",
"- **Moody and cinematic:** muted greens, teal shadows, warm skin. Suits receptions and evening portraits.",
"Look at the album templates you plan to use. A golden Karizma background calls for warm grading, while a white floral template suits a clean look. Our [wedding album colour themes](/blog/wedding-album-colour-themes/) guide helps match the two.",
"## Building a grade in Lightroom or Camera Raw",
"### Tone curve",
"A gentle S-curve adds contrast. Lifting the bottom-left point slightly gives a soft, matte black that many couples like. Keep it subtle; heavy fades make prints look washed out.",
"### HSL and colour mixer",
"Use the Orange slider carefully because it controls skin. Reduce saturation of distracting greens and blues in the background, and shift greens slightly toward yellow for a warmer feel.",
"### Colour grading wheels",
"Add a little warmth to highlights and a touch of teal or neutral to shadows. Balance around 0 to 20 for most wedding work. Save the result as a preset so every photo starts from the same point.",
"## Applying the grade consistently",
"Apply the preset to all selected photos, then work event by event. Ceremony photos under yellow lights need different base corrections than outdoor portraits, but the grade on top stays the same. Use the reference view or compare mode to place a hero image beside each photo you adjust.",
"## Grading inside Photoshop",
"If photos are already placed in album PSDs, add a Curves or Color Lookup adjustment layer at the top of the photo stack and clip it to the photo, not the whole sheet. Grading the entire sheet would also shift the template background and ornaments, which usually looks wrong.",
"### Using LUTs",
"Color Lookup adjustment layers can apply a LUT file. Reduce the layer opacity to 40 to 70 percent so the effect is gentle and skin stays natural.",
"## Protecting skin tones",
"Indian skin tones vary widely, and heavy grading can make them grey, orange or green. Check faces at 100 percent zoom after every change. If skin shifts, add a masked Hue/Saturation layer to pull it back.",
"## Checking before print",
"A calibrated monitor is essential; read our [monitor calibration guide](/blog/monitor-calibration-for-album-designers/). Order a test print of one graded spread before the full album, and keep the grade a little lighter than you think, because prints are usually darker than screens.",
"## Templates that suit each look",
"For warm grades, try [golden album PSDs](/tag/golden-album-psd/) and [Karizma album PSDs](/tag/karizma-album-psd/). For clean looks, the [floral album PSDs](/tag/floral-album-psd/) and [12x36 wedding album sheets](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) offer lighter backgrounds.",
]),
})
